WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:03.311
We entered all three FinEval tasks:

2
00:00:03.311 --> 00:00:08.590
Greek financial named entity recognition,
Japanese implicit communication

3
00:00:08.590 --> 00:00:12.680
recognition, and multilingual financial
question answering.

4
00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:18.002
ADML ranked first in Greek, joint first in
Japanese, and second in question

5
00:00:18.002 --> 00:00:18.989
answering.

6
00:00:19.369 --> 00:00:25.124
This FinNLP presentation accompanies our
question answering poster.

7
00:00:25.124 --> 00:00:30.122
It covers the agentic research workflow, the
models we tried across all three

8
00:00:30.122 --> 00:00:33.108
tasks, and the systems we submitted.

9
00:00:33.108 --> 00:00:38.149
Our results were strong, but the final
systems were much simpler than those

10
00:00:38.149 --> 00:00:40.353
we had spent the competition developing.

11
00:00:41.203 --> 00:00:46.157
Our agentic research loop started with the
data and the scoring code.

12
00:00:46.157 --> 00:00:51.956
Reproduce a baseline, inspect failures,
propose a hypothesis, implement an

13
00:00:51.956 --> 00:00:54.725
experiment, and decide whether to keep the
change.

14
00:00:54.725 --> 00:00:58.369
We recorded negative results alongside
improvements.

15
00:00:58.749 --> 00:01:04.558
Validation reflected the task: related
entity surfaces for Greek, held out

16
00:01:04.558 --> 00:01:09.166
sources for Japanese, and held out companies
or reporting periods for question

17
00:01:09.166 --> 00:01:14.034
answering. We tracked the average score,
which examples were corrected, which

18
00:01:14.034 --> 00:01:18.883
were harmed, and whether improvements
carried over to another source or period.

19
00:01:19.458 --> 00:01:24.153
The archived repository contains two hundred
and nine scripts, thirty two

20
00:01:24.153 --> 00:01:30.014
source modules, ninety nine test files, and
twenty five preregistration documents.

21
00:01:30.864 --> 00:01:37.182
For Greek entities, we explored GreekBERT,
legal encoders, XLM-R, and GLiNER

22
00:01:37.182 --> 00:01:41.920
proposals, with separate numeric processing
and conservative span repairs.

23
00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:47.610
For Japanese intent, we investigated BERT
ensembles, RoBERTa, ModernBERT,

24
00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:52.305
and a nine billion parameter Nemotron model,
alongside domain adaptation

25
00:01:52.305 --> 00:01:54.032
and ordinal decoding.

26
00:01:54.564 --> 00:01:59.713
For question answering, trials included an
Eva four billion parameter adapter,

27
00:01:59.713 --> 00:02:03.196
Qwen answer generators, and larger fact
planners.

28
00:02:03.196 --> 00:02:08.238
The structured approach tracked periods,
units, arithmetic, and evidence

29
00:02:08.238 --> 00:02:10.706
before considering small answer edits.

30
00:02:11.086 --> 00:02:14.450
We rejected many candidates after
validation.

31
00:02:14.450 --> 00:02:18.604
ModernBERT made every Japanese validation
fold worse.

32
00:02:18.604 --> 00:02:23.494
A Qwen fact filter improved average fact set
F one, but harmed one issuer

33
00:02:23.494 --> 00:02:27.886
beyond our allowed threshold. Conservative
Greek span consensus corrected

34
00:02:27.886 --> 00:02:31.521
four validation examples without introducing
a new error.

35
00:02:31.521 --> 00:02:36.367
We had inspected that validation set
repeatedly, so these gains still needed

36
00:02:36.367 --> 00:02:37.995
confirmation on fresh data.

37
00:02:38.845 --> 00:02:43.388
The question answering experiment
illustrates the central difficulty.

38
00:02:43.388 --> 00:02:48.148
On one hundred and ninety two historical
examples, the grounded baseline

39
00:02:48.148 --> 00:02:51.015
scored about point three six five in
ROUGE-1.

40
00:02:51.015 --> 00:02:55.677
An oracle, allowed to see the reference
answer and choose the best sparse

41
00:02:55.677 --> 00:02:58.753
edit, reached about point four zero three.

42
00:02:59.133 --> 00:03:03.730
Our nested company held out selector chose
to make no edits, retaining the

43
00:03:03.730 --> 00:03:08.382
baseline. In a separate temporal transfer
test, all four changed answers

44
00:03:08.382 --> 00:03:12.601
became worse. The oracle showed that useful
edits existed.

45
00:03:12.601 --> 00:03:16.344
Choosing them for a new company or reporting
period remained the problem,

46
00:03:16.344 --> 00:03:19.530
and keeping the baseline was the best
decision from validation.

47
00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:23.875
The final data audit changed our competition
strategy.

48
00:03:23.875 --> 00:03:29.587
Every Greek test query, and all seventy six
question answering queries, matched

49
00:03:29.587 --> 00:03:35.969
earlier public inputs. The organizers
allowed external data, so we retrieved

50
00:03:35.969 --> 00:03:40.037
the matching public reference answers for
those two tasks.

51
00:03:40.037 --> 00:03:45.272
For Japanese intent, we used deterministic
rules based on explicit commitment

52
00:03:45.272 --> 00:03:46.938
and refusal cues.

53
00:03:47.318 --> 00:03:50.185
The final runner used no neural model.

54
00:03:50.185 --> 00:03:55.118
Question answering scored almost one on the
public portion and point three

55
00:03:55.118 --> 00:03:58.948
seven three on the private portion, placing
second.

56
00:03:58.948 --> 00:04:02.517
The gap suggests that the reference answers
changed between versions.

57
00:04:02.517 --> 00:04:06.131
We could not check this directly because the
private reference answers were

58
00:04:06.131 --> 00:04:07.190
unavailable.

59
00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:12.367
The disappointment was that the final
benchmark gave us little opportunity

60
00:04:12.367 --> 00:04:15.786
to test the models we had developed for new
inputs.

61
00:04:15.786 --> 00:04:20.978
We submitted retrieval for Greek and
question answering, and a semantic rubric

62
00:04:20.978 --> 00:04:26.885
for Japanese. The hidden test therefore left
the broader model work unresolved.

63
00:04:27.265 --> 00:04:30.640
For future tasks, we would keep three
practices:

64
00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:36.006
check the data and reference versions, test
on new sources and periods, and

65
00:04:36.006 --> 00:04:38.343
keep a record of failed experiments.

66
00:04:38.343 --> 00:04:43.167
Agents helped us build and test candidates,
and the validation results guided

67
00:04:43.167 --> 00:04:46.175
what we kept. Thank you.
